# Heart Rate

The Heart Rate report answers the question "what does my heart rate do through
the day, and what does my glucose do at the same time?". It shows your heart
rate through each day, with your glucose on the same row.

<Video id="heart-rate" />

## What it shows

### The key figures

| Figure | What it shows |
|--------|---------------|
| **Average** | Your average heart rate in the period, in beats per minute (bpm) |
| **Resting estimate** | An estimate of your resting heart rate. Only one heart rate reading in ten is lower than this value. |
| **Min / Max** | Your lowest and highest heart rate in the period |
| **Readings** | The number of heart rate readings |

### Heart Rate Actogram

The actogram has one row for each day. The newest day is at the top.

- **The small dots** are your heart rate. A higher dot means a faster heart
  rate.
- **The line and the larger dots** are your glucose. The colour of each glucose
  dot shows the range of that reading.

Each row covers 48 hours: the day itself, and then the next day in a paler
colour. This lets you follow an evening into the night without a break.

Move the pointer over a row to see the heart rate and the glucose at that time.

The app shows glucose values in mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

## How to use it

The actogram shows 14 days at a time. To compare the same hours one week apart:

1. Find a time with a high heart rate, for example during exercise. Look at the
   glucose before and after.
2. Select the down arrow under the rows. The rows move one week back.
3. Find the same day of the week. Compare the heart rate and the glucose at the
   same time.
4. To move one week forward again, select the up arrow above the rows.

You can also use the keyboard. Select the actogram, and then press the up or
down arrow key to move one day. Hold Shift to move one week, or Alt to move 30
days.

To change the period, select the date range at the top of the report, select a
period, and then select **Apply Filters**.

## Good to know

- The figures at the top use only the period you chose. The actogram can also
  show the days before the period, when you move back.
